AB  - Generative large language models (LLMs) possess considerable potential for mitigating psychological distress among adolescents and advancing mental health education. Existing mental health-oriented large models primarily concentrate on acquiring empathetic capabilities, yet they still struggle to produce counseling responses that combine professional guidance with clear objectives for each dialogue turn. In addition, a significant gap exists between the linguistic expressions used by psychology experts and those of the general population, and this discrepancy directly affects users' willingness to express emotions. To tackle these challenges, this article introduces an iterative training framework that integrates dual perspectives from both counselors and adolescent clients. The framework first trains the model to identify core user needs and establish phased counseling goals based on user input. It then employs contrastive learning techniques to ensure the generated content maintains internal consistency in both linguistic style and counseling objectives. The study also provides an in-depth examination of the dynamic psychological evolution of adolescents after receiving multiple rounds of large model-based counseling. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves excellent performance across multiple key metrics, including credibility, empathy, respectfulness, and depth of guidance. Human-machine dialogue mental health LLMs are undergoing a paradigm shift from passive empathy to active goal-directed intervention.
