TI  - Speech-Integrated Modeling for Behavioral Coding in Counseling
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE 26TH ANNUAL MEETING OF THE SPECIAL INTEREST GROUP ON DISCOURSE AND DIALOGUE
LA  - English
CP  - 26th Special Interest Group on Discourse and Dialogue Meeting-SIGDIAL-Annual
AB  - Counseling and psychotherapy are predominantly conducted in spoken form, yet computational models to analyze these conversations often use only text transcripts as a main data source. This dependency overlooks important vocal cues such as tone, pitch, and prosody, hence restricting the models' capacity to fully capture the dynamics of counselor-client interactions. We introduce Motivational Interviewing with Speech Cues (MISQ), a simple yet effective framework for enhancing the analysis of psychotherapy conversations that uses a large language model, a lightweight adapter, and a speech encoder to directly integrate speech cues. Our experiments show that MISQ consistently outperforms (similar to 5% relative improvement) approaches that omit or use speech indirectly, highlighting the essential role of speech in accurately capturing counselor and client behaviors.
