AB  - Longitudinal behavioral change coaching requires sustained, relationship-dependent support across weeks, yet most LLM-based conversational agents are optimized for short, loosely structured interactions. In extended use, such agents frequently lose track of evolving goals and unresolved barriers, resulting in repetitive questioning and degraded counseling structure. We present a multi-LLM system that structures longitudinal coaching as a controlled process grounded in evidence-based behavioral strategies. This system consists of multiple LLM-based agents with defined roles. A summary agent maintains a persistent coaching state aligned with SMART goal principles. At the same time, an automated signal generator agent converts this state with recent chat history into compact steering instructions injected into a dialogue agent's prompt for response. By separating stable counseling principles from dynamic session guidance, the system enables proactive agenda management without reliance on unbounded conversational history. We implement a web-based prototype featuring a multi-page coaching interface and a database. We validate the proposed system with this prototype in a multi-session, multiuser study, comparing against prompt-only and memory-based baseline conditions. The results suggest that the proposed multi-agent system consistently achieves high-quality longitudinal coaching performance across multiple dimensions. More broadly, our architecture offers a reusable design pattern in supporting sustained coaching trajectories. It provides a practical foundation for future digital health coaching systems that require reliable continuity, goal tracking, and follow-up.
