AB  - Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have triggered extensive research into constructing LLMs for human communication. Understanding human intentions, such as the degree to which an individual aligns with certain thoughts or objectives, is crucial in interpersonal communication. However, despite notable technological progress, narrowing the gap between current LLM capabilities and the ideal conversational AI that comprehends these nuanced human intentions remains challenging. Failure to consider human intentions in LLM-based conversations may lead to frustrating or indifferent user experiences. This study proposes a novel method for effectively estimating human intentions from persona information using LLM agents. Persona information such as age, gender, job, and personality traits, serve as a valuable context for understanding user intentions. The method involves both forward and backward estimation to verify outputs. By backwardly estimating persona information solely from inferred human intentions and evaluating the proximity between the backward-estimated persona and the originally provided persona, we refine the alignment of intentions. To guide our exploration, we address two primary research questions. First, quantifying the extent of accuracy improvement using our proposed method compared to direct LLM usage (Baseline), and Second, exploring the effectiveness of our method across various questions. In an experiment with 274 persona sets, our proposed method consistently demonstrated a significant 12% or more reduction in Mean Absolute Error for each question when applied multiple times, surpassing the baseline LLM. The proposed method holds promise for more accurate and empathetic interactions, addressing previous inaccuracies in understanding these nuanced human intentions by LLM. Future work will extend the evaluation to other tasks like emotion prediction and explore applications in other domains.
