KW  - Human-AI Interaction
AB  - The increasing use of large language models is reshaping education. However, it remains an open question whether text-based interfaces are sufficient, or if learners can benefit more from embodied conversational agents (ECAs) that enable more natural, human-like interactions with artificial intelligence. In the context of career counseling-a domain where personalized guidance is vital but difficult to scale-ECAs offer promising support by complementing human advisors. A key factor in the successful adoption of such systems is users' trust.
   This study examines how ECAs influence students' trust in AI-assisted career counseling, comparing real-time interactions with prerecorded video-based agents. The findings revealed that measurement scales were more reliable in the real-time condition. However, participants generally rated video-based ECAs more favorably. Interestingly, male ECAs received higher trust ratings across most measures, while female ECAs in real-time interactions received the lowest ratings, suggesting a negative interaction between modality and agent gender. These results emphasize the importance of both interaction format and gender representation in designing trustworthy ECAs for educational and counseling contexts.
