TI  - Towards collaborative intelligence: Reimagining human-agent co-design in traditional craft design through multi-agent Empathic Handover
T2  - CODESIGN-INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF COCREATION IN DESIGN AND THE ARTS
LA  - English
KW  - Large Language Models (LLMs)
KW  - multi-agents system
KW  - co-design
KW  - Empathic Handover
KW  - human-AI collaboration
KW  - modernisation of traditional handicrafts
AB  - The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) is transforming multi-agent systems (MAS) from reinforcement learning approaches to role-driven architectures, significantly expanding the horizons for human - agent collaboration. However, a persistent challenge in co-design remains the lack of systematic mechanisms for agents to achieve continuous empathy and nuanced contextual understanding - elements vital to human-centred design. This study addresses this gap by investigating the efficacy of MAS roles in traditional handicraft co-design, exploring their functional mechanisms and capability limits. We establish a mixed-methods, dual-track framework comparing human stakeholders with multi-agent roles, using the modernisation of Mongolian leather-carving as a high-context test case. Crucially, the 'Empathic Handover' (EH) framework is introduced as a theoretical and practical bridge to integrate agents into this collaborative process. The results demonstrate that the agent is capable of generating adaptive feedback by simulating the user's perspective, thereby enhancing the contextual relevance of the design. The results reveal a human - agent cognitive complementarity: while agents offer rapid, knowledge-recombination-based creativity, humans retain irreplaceable advantages in embodied cognition and tactile expertise. Despite current constraints in LLM parameter settings, this research establishes a robust methodology for future human-agent co-creation in culturally rich, high-context domains.
