KW  - Role-playing
KW  - VALIDATION
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly capable of complex socio-affective behaviors, yet their underlying psychological dispositions remain poorly understood. Prior research has primarily focused on general personality assessments, with limited attention to the risks posed by maladaptive traits. We introduce Dark-Affective Bench, a multidimensional evaluation framework for quantifying maladaptive personality traits and affective tendencies exhibited by pre-trained LLMs in diverse contexts. The benchmark consists of 159 items covering four core domains and nine specific psychological or affective constructs. We evaluate seven widely used LLMs, including variants from LLaMA-3, DeepSeek-V3, ChatGPT, and Qwen. All models exhibit maladaptive tendencies to some extent. Notably, empathy deficits are consistent across all models, while open-source architectures exhibit higher levels of aggression, highlighting the need for targeted affective alignment. Importantly, these tendencies vary significantly with alignment strategies rather than model scale. Our findings provide a principled framework for assessing the psychological safety of LLMs and inform the development of emotionally intelligent, ethically aligned AI systems.
