AB  - This experimental study examined how a chatbot's response style (empathetic versus informative), agent identity (generic versus anthropomorphized), and gender match (same versus different user/chatbot gender) influence users' perceptions of interactions with chatbot and self-disclosure of depressive experiences. We utilized a 2 & times; 2 & times; 2 between-subjects experimental design with 417 American adult participants interacting with a text-based chatbot. Results showed that participants exposed to empathetic responses reported greater willingness to disclose depressive experiences. Neither the anthropomorphic agent's identity nor gender match had a significant effect on the outcomes. Additionally, although trust positively predicts self-disclosure and satisfaction, it did not mediate the effects of empathetic responses on users' satisfaction. These findings underscore the significance of emotional responsiveness compared to an informational approach in human-machine health communication. Implications for affective computing and digital mental health assistance tools are discussed.
